#version 450
//! Attention scores over a **backward sliding window**, stored as a band, with the
//! relative-position term and the logit cap fused in.
//!
//! `scores[h][q][j] = tanh(t / cap) * cap` where
//! `t = Q[h,.,q] . K[h,.,k] + Q[h,.,q] . table[h][j + 1]` and `k = q - (band - 1) + j`.
//!
//! Twelve uses, one per conformer layer of Gemma 4's audio tower. See `nets::gemma4_audio`.
//!
//! # The band is the layout, not an optimisation applied to a square map
//!
//! The tower attends `0 <= q - k <= 11`, so the output is `[heads, T, band]` with `band` 12 and
//! every slot a pair the mask admits. At the 750-token cap a square `[heads, T, T]` map is 31x
//! the memory and 31x the arithmetic with 1.6% of its slots live. `p.out_w` is therefore the
//! **band width**, not the key count - which is why the sequence stride here comes from
//! `p.in_h * p.in_w` and not from `p.out_w` the way `attn_scores.comp` reads it.
//!
//! Nothing downstream needs a windowed softmax: a filled 12-wide row is an ordinary softmax
//! domain, so the plain `softmax.comp` normalises this untouched.
//!
//! # The guard
//!
//! `j + q + 1 >= band`, with the subtraction only after it. **This mirrors `band_key_in` in
//! `nets/gemma4_audio.rs`** - same shape, same order - and `nets::reference::attn_scores_banded`
//! calls that function rather than restating it, so this line is the only place in the tree
//! where the expression is written out a second time. Keep them identical.
//!
//! It mirrors `band_key_in(p.kh, ..)` and not `band_key(..)`, which is the same guard frozen at
//! the tower's own span of twelve. This op is parameterised by `Push::kh`, so pinning it to the
//! constant would be right at band 12 and wrong at every other width - which is exactly the bug
//! the CPU arm had, and it read as a plausible band rather than as a failure.
//!
//! It is written this way because four rearrangements were tried and every one is wrong:
//!
//! * `k >= 0` is vacuous for an unsigned `k`; the compiler discards it.
//! * `j >= band - 1 - q` underflows for `q > 11`, wrapping to about 4.29e9 so the guard rejects
//!   the whole band - correct on twelve rows and empty on the 738 after them.
//! * Computing `k`, using it, and masking the result afterwards has already read out of bounds.
//!   The guard has to gate the **read**.
//! * `k < T` alone is safe here, where wrap is defined, but the CPU arm cannot share it: the
//!   same subtraction panics in a Rust debug build, which is what every parity run is.
//!
//! The form above is purely additive, so there is nothing to wrap on either side.
//!
//! `k < stride` is kept as the upper bound. `band_key` guards the lower bound only, and the
//! upper is free *solely* because right context is zero, so `k <= q < T`. It is retained
//! because that is a property of this tower rather than of the expression.
//!
//! # The relative term is content-dependent, and in band coordinates it needs no skew
//!
//! `relative_k_proj(sinusoid)`, dot-producted against the query Transformer-XL style rather than
//! added as a bias, so it cannot be precomputed per `(q, k)`. The displacement `q - k` is
//! `band - 1 - j`, so the table column is `j + 1` **independent of `q`** - which is what removes
//! the export's `_rel_shift` pad/reshape/slice entirely. See `gemma4_audio::rel_column`.
//!
//! `p.weight` is the table as `[heads, offsets, head_dim]`, transposed by the converter from the
//! export's `[heads, head_dim, offsets]` so that `head_dim` - the axis this contracts over - is
//! contiguous. `p.kw` is the offset count, 13 for a 12-wide band; column 0 is displacement 12,
//! which the mask never admits.
//!
//! # There is no scale here, and that is deliberate
//!
//! `p.param0_bits` is passed through so the oracle and this agree field for field, but
//! `Builder::attn_scores_banded` hardcodes it to 1.0 and takes no scale argument.
//!
//! The export's query scale is `(head_dim ** -0.5) / ln 2 = 0.127517431974411`
//! (`val_279`; note the `/ ln 2`, so it is *not* `1/sqrt(128) = 0.088388`).
//! `maml_convert.py` folds it into `softplus(per_dim_scale)` and emits the product as a
//! per-layer `[d_model, 1, 1]` `QueryScale` tensor, so Q arrives here already scaled.
//!
//! It could not be applied here in any case: `softplus(per_dim_scale)` varies per channel, and
//! `p.param0_bits` is one float. Applying the scalar part alone would be wrong twice over.
//! Compare `Builder::attn_scores_prescaled`, which exists for the same reason after the vision
//! tower shipped every score divided by eight.
//!
//! # The cap is fused, and the fill goes in after it
//!
//! `p.param1_bits` is `attention_logit_cap`. It applies to the sum of both terms and **before**
//! the mask, so it cannot be a separate `softcap.comp` pass: that pass would run over a band
//! already holding the sentinel and map it to `tanh(-1310.08) * 50 = -50`, a finite weight the
//! softmax would then happily include. Fusing puts the sentinel in after the cap, which is
//! correct by construction rather than by remembering the order.
//!
//! Dead slots get `-65504.0`, fp16's most negative finite value - `nets::gemma4_audio::MASK_FILL`.
//! Not `-1e9`, which saturates to `-inf` in this fp16 arena and turns a fully masked row into
//! `0/0 = NaN`.

#include "common.glsl"

void main() {
    uint index = global_index();
    if (index >= p.count) {
        return;
    }
    // The output is `[heads, queries, band]`, so the third field is the band column and not a
    // key. `key` is derived from it below.
    uint head, query, column;
    unpack(index, head, query, column);

    uint head_dim = p.in_c / p.group;
    // Q and K are `[d_model, 1, T]`, so this is T: one channel every T elements. `score_map`
    // refuses a height above one, which is what makes the two spellings the same number.
    uint stride = p.in_h * p.in_w;
    uint band = p.kh;

    // Mirrors `band_key_in` in `nets/gemma4_audio.rs`: the guard is `j + q + 1 >= band` and the
    // subtraction happens only after it has proved the result non-negative. The smallest sum the
    // guard admits is exactly `band`, so `query + column + 1 - band` cannot wrap even at the
    // boundary. Do not rearrange it - see the header for the four forms that are wrong.
    float score = -65504.0;
    if (column + query + 1u >= band) {
        uint key = query + column + 1u - band;
        // The upper bound, free only because right context is zero. Kept anyway.
        if (key < stride) {
            uint base = head * head_dim * stride;
            // Column `j` of the band is relative offset `j + 1`, whatever `query` is.
            uint row = p.weight + (head * p.kw + column + 1u) * head_dim;
            float total = 0.0;
            for (uint d = 0u; d < head_dim; d++) {
                uint channel = base + d * stride;
                // One load of Q serves both terms; they contract over the same axis.
                float q = float(arena[p.in0 + channel + query]);
                total += q * float(arena[p.in1 + channel + key]);
                total += q * float(weights[row + d]);
            }
            total *= uintBitsToFloat(p.param0_bits);
            float cap = uintBitsToFloat(p.param1_bits);
            score = tanh(total / cap) * cap;
        }
    }
    arena[p.out_at + index] = float16_t(score);
}
